{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target names 'inheritance' of an 'absence of a starting offset,' but this is not a substantive dependence, reduction, shared-mechanism, or inheritance relation connecting 07e5ec535396 to its neighbourhood. Inheriting an absence is a negative claim, not a causal mechanism linking the endpoints: it does not show how the 792-digit total depends on, reduces to, or shares a mechanism with the '300 pages' result. The target merely summarizes the two endpoints (300 pages and a 792-digit count) and attaches the label 'compatible/inheriting' without stating what the inherited mechanism IS or how it causally produces the connection. The only operative content is the refutation condition (front matter inclusion shifts the offset), which describes a contradiction, not a positive relation.","target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"The target names 'sharing the mechanism of single-number-per-side allocation,' but this phrase is a restatement of the arithmetic (792 digits / ~2.64 digits per page ≈ 300), not a genuine causal mechanism that connects 07e5ec535396 to its neighbourhood. Saying the two endpoints 'share' the same allocation rule does not establish a dependence, reduction, or inheritance relation between them; it merely asserts that both use the same convention, which is a compatibility claim so thin it collapses into a summary of the endpoints. The target fails to state what the mechanism IS in causal terms (e.g., how the digit sequence is generated from the page-numbering process such that 792 digits necessarily yield 300 pages) and instead labels the shared convention as the mechanism.","target_alias":"SRC_002"}]}